{
  "concept": "A Fibonacci heap is a collection of min-heap-ordered trees whose roots live in a circular doubly linked root list with an H.min pointer, and the abstract tree view is just shorthand for an explicit pointer structure where every node has parent, child, left, and right pointers.",
  "figureType": "network_diagram",
  "elements": [
    "panel (a) abstract tree view",
    "panel (b) explicit pointer diagram",
    "root nodes 23, 7, 3, 17, 24 (gray circles) connected by a dashed horizontal line (panel a) or by paired directed arrows forming a circular doubly linked list (panel b)",
    "H.min arrow pointing down to root node 3",
    "tree edges from 3 to children 18, 52, 38; from 18 to 39; from 38 to 41; from 17 to 30; from 24 to 26 and 46; from 26 to 35",
    "marked nodes 18, 39, 26 drawn as filled black circles with white numerals",
    "panel (b): paired forward/backward directed arrows between siblings in each child list (18↔52↔38, 26↔46) and self-loop arrows on singleton children (30, 39, 41, 35)",
    "panel (b): bidirectional parent/child arrows between each parent and its child list",
    "labels (a) and (b) at left of each panel"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Representation",
      "options": ["Tree view (a)", "Pointer view (b)"],
      "default": "Tree view (a)",
      "teaches": "The dashed root-list line and tree edges of the abstract view are shorthand for circular doubly linked lists and explicit parent/child pointers — both panels describe exactly the same heap"
    },
    {
      "id": "inspectNode",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Every node carries the same four pointer fields — parent, child, left sibling, right sibling — and clicking a node reveals how those four pointers wire it into both its sibling ring and the tree above and below it"
    },
    {
      "id": "insertAnimation",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "FIB-HEAP-INSERT",
      "loop": false,
      "teaches": "Insertion is O(1): a new singleton node is spliced into the root list by rewiring a constant number of left/right pointers, and H.min is updated only if the new key beats the current minimum"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The abstract heap",
      "narration": "Five min-heap-ordered trees share one root list (dashed line), and H.min points at the smallest root, 3. Black nodes 18, 39, and 26 are marked — each has lost a child since being linked to its parent.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Tree view (a)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Same heap, real pointers",
      "narration": "Switch to the pointer view: the dashed line becomes a circular doubly linked list of roots, and each group of children is its own circular doubly linked list, with bidirectional parent/child arrows.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Pointer view (b)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Inspect a node's four pointers",
      "narration": "Click any node — for example 3 or 18 — to highlight its parent, child, left, and right pointers, and see how the same four fields serve both the root list and every child list.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Pointer view (b)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Constant-time insert",
      "narration": "Watch FIB-HEAP-INSERT splice a new node into the root list next to H.min by rewiring a handful of pointers — no tree restructuring at all — and see H.min move only when the new key is smaller than 3.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Pointer view (b)" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The Fibonacci heap's power comes from lazy structure: roots and siblings live in circular doubly linked lists, so inserting a node or adding a tree to the root list costs only a constant number of pointer changes.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two stacked renderings of the same heap, swapped by the toggle. Root row left-to-right: 23, 7, 3, 17, 24, with H.min arrow above node 3. Children: 3 → {18, 52, 38}, 18 → 39, 38 → 41, 17 → 30, 24 → {26, 46}, 26 → 35. Marked nodes 18, 39, 26 filled black with white text; all others light gray with black text. Tree view: dashed horizontal line through the roots, plain straight parent-child edges. Pointer view: each sibling group drawn with paired curved directed arrows (clockwise/counter-clockwise) forming a ring, singleton children get self-loop arrows, and parent-child links are paired vertical arrows. On node click, highlight the clicked node plus its four pointer arrows in distinct colors with small labels p, child, left, right. Insert animation: a new node (e.g. key 21, then optionally key 2) fades in beside the root list, arrows rewire to splice it in, and the H.min arrow slides to it only if its key < 3."
}

